 180: The adapter supplies storage; the shared module supplies every judgement. **Constraint: that file
 181: has zero non-relative imports and touches no Deno global**, because `tests/at/tsconfig.json` compiles
 182: it under `strict` with `skipLibCheck: false`, `types: ["node"]` and no DOM.
 183: 
 184: ### D5 â€” Schema shape
 185: 
 186: Two enums: `account_type` (`ngo`, `volunteer`, `platform_admin`) and `org_role` (`admin`, `member`).
 187: 
 188: - `public.accounts` â€” primary key `id uuid references auth.users(id) on delete cascade`,
 189:   `account_type` not null, `created_at`. **One row per auth user is what makes "one account holds
 190:   exactly one global type" structural** rather than a rule someone remembers.
 191: - `public.organizations` â€” `id`, `name`, `created_at`.
 192: - `public.org_memberships` â€” `(org_id, account_id)` primary key, `role org_role` not null.
 193: - `public.acknowledgments` â€” `account_id`, `kind`, `acknowledged_at`, `ip inet`, `text_version`.
 194:   **Exactly the three fields AT-001.01 names.** Name / title / authority attestation is AT-001.19,
 195:   which the manifest's acknowledgment-identity deliverable owns â€” adding those columns now would be
 196:   speculation.
 197: - `public.has_platform_acknowledgment(account_id) returns boolean` â€” AT-001.01 says the
 198:   acknowledgment is required *"before any project creation is possible"*, and no projects table
 199:   exists. The predicate is the observable form of that clause; the future project path calls it.
 200:   **[F3] It must DISCRIMINATE, and the test proves that**: false for an authenticated user who has
 201:   not completed signup, true after. A constant-true implementation then fails AT-001.01 instead of
 202:   satisfying it. What this leaf cannot do is enforce the clause â€” nothing in the tree creates a
 203:   project, and building project creation belongs to another requirement entirely. Section 4 says so
 204:   in those words rather than letting the predicate's existence imply otherwise.
 205: 
 206: - **[F5] `public.complete_signup(â€¦)` â€” the one transactional write path.** The plan promised four
 207:   writes "in one transaction" and had arranged no transaction: separate Data API calls are separate
 208:   transactions, so a partial failure could leave an account with no organisation, membership or
 209:   acknowledgment. This function performs all four writes and the edge function calls it once â€” one
 210:   round trip, one implicit transaction, all four rows or none. Step 7 proves it by forcing a
 211:   completion that fails partway and showing no partial state survives.
 212:   **It re-checks the account type itself and raises on `platform_admin` [F6].** That is the one
 213:   place the database repeats a decision the shared module already makes, and it is deliberate: see
 214:   below.
 215: 
 216: - **[added in sitting 3] `public.create_organization(â€¦)` â€” the same reasoning, applied to the
 217:   operation F4 added.** Creating an organisation is two writes, the organisation and its `admin`
 218:   membership. As two Data API calls that is two transactions, and a failure between them orphans an
 219:   organisation with no admin â€” **the exact defect F5 ruled against, reappearing inside the operation
 220:   F4 introduced.** The executor built it as a fifth database object rather than letting the new
 221:   operation reinstate the old defect; **I rule that correct.** It performs the two writes and decides
 222:   nothing: the NGO-only refusal stays in the shared module, so AT-001.06 still exercises shipped
 223:   TypeScript rather than a rule buried in SQL.
 224: 
 225: - **[found in sitting 3, by measuring rather than by reading] The privilege posture is part of the
 226:   schema, not an afterthought.** Two facts were established against the live database and both
 227:   changed the migration. PostgreSQL grants `EXECUTE` on a new function to `PUBLIC` by default, so
 228:   every function here is explicitly revoked from `PUBLIC` before being granted to `service_role` â€”
 229:   without that, anyone holding only the publishable key could have called `complete_signup`. And
 230:   `service_role`'s `BYPASSRLS` is not a table privilege, so it holds `SELECT` on `accounts` and
 231:   **INSERT on nothing**; all writing goes through `SECURITY DEFINER` functions with
 232:   `set search_path = ''`. Recorded here because the obvious repair â€” granting the service role INSERT
 233:   â€” is the one that must not be made: see step 7(g).
 234: 
 235: **No trigger on `auth.users`.** The account row is created by the edge function. A user who
 236: authenticated but never completed signup is a real and honest state â€” it is literally what
 237: AT-001.04 (deliverable D1 leaf L2) tests for. A trigger here would be a footgun whose failure mode
 238: is a 500 inside Supabase Auth.
 239: 
 240: **Row-level security is enabled on every new table, and only the policies these four tests need are
 241: added.** Everything else stays denied, which is both the minimal change and the safe default. The
 242: full tenant-isolation policy set belongs to the manifest's tenant-isolation deliverable and is not
 243: built here. (This plan numbers its own decisions D1â€“D8; the manifest numbers its deliverables the
 244: same way. Where the two could be confused, the manifest's are named in words.)
 245: 
 246: **[F6] What the missing insert policy does and does not stop â€” the earlier wording here was false.**
 247: It stops the anon and authenticated key paths: a browser holding the public key cannot insert into
 248: `accounts` at all, and step 7 proves that. It stops **nothing** on the service-role path, and the
 249: public edge function must write with exactly that authority â€” so row-level security is not in the
